As Cloud Engineers, we automate infrastructure, troubleshoot environments, and build CI/CD pipelines—but many of us only scratch the surface of what Git can do.

If your Git usage looks like this:
✅ git clone
✅ git add . && git commit -m "stuff"
✅ git push
…then you’re missing out on serious time-saving features!

This weekend, I dove into learning some next-level Git commands that every Cloud Engineer should know:

🔁 git rebase – Keeps your commit history clean. Great for prepping PRs and collaborative workflows.
📦 git stash – Save your uncommitted work without cluttering your branch. Perfect when switching tasks mid-way.
🔍 git log --oneline --graph – Visualize your commit tree and branch flow like a pro. Super helpful when dealing with complex merges.

🧠 Why bother? Because in a cloud-native world where we’re managing IaC, containers, and automation scripts, a strong Git workflow helps prevent mistakes, supports rollback strategies, and boosts team collaboration.

🎯 Bonus tools and resources to explore next:

- git bisect for fast debugging
- git worktree for working on multiple branches    
